<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Identifying an irrational number from a given list of real numbers</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Consider the following four numbers:</p>
<p>\[ {@d1@}, \quad {@d2@}, \quad {@d3@}, \quad {@d4@} \]</p>
<p>Which one of these numbers is <b>irrational</b>? <i>(Type a square root such as \(\sqrt{5}\) as <code>sqrt(5)</code>, or \(2\sqrt{3}\) as <code>2*sqrt(3)</code>.)</i></p>
<p>Irrational number = [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p><b>Worked Solution:</b></p>

<p>A number is <b>rational</b> if it can be written as a fraction \(\displaystyle \frac{p}{q}\) where \(p\) and \(q\) are integers and \(q \neq 0\) (its decimal expansion either terminates or repeats). A number is <b>irrational</b> if it cannot be written as a ratio of two integers.</p>

<p>Check each of the four numbers:</p>
<ul>
  <li>\({@a1@}\) is an integer, which can be written as \(\displaystyle \frac{{@a1@}}{1}\), so it is <b>rational</b>.</li>
  <li>\({@a2@}\) is already a ratio of two integers, so it is <b>rational</b>.</li>
  <li>\(\sqrt{{@a5_sq@}}\) has a square root symbol, but \({@a5_sq@} = {@a5@}^2\) is a perfect square, so \(\sqrt{{@a5_sq@}} = {@a5@} = \displaystyle \frac{{@a5@}}{1}\) is <b>rational</b>.</li>
  <li>\(\sqrt{{@n3@}}\) is <b>irrational</b> because \({@n3@}\) is not a perfect square, so \(\sqrt{{@n3@}}\) cannot be simplified to an integer or a fraction of integers.</li>
</ul>

<p><b>Key takeaway:</b> Always simplify any radical before deciding whether it is rational or irrational.</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px; font-size: 1.2em;">\(\displaystyle \boxed{ \text{Irrational number} = \sqrt{{@n3@}} }\)</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
